Subject: [PATCH] dsd: decimate the PCM output down to a rate the DAC accepts

The DSD to PCM conversion (dsd2pcm) always yields DSD rate / 8: 352.8 kHz
for DSD64, 705.6 kHz for DSD128. A DAC that stops at 192 kHz or less
refuses that rate and the track does not play, or plays at the wrong
pitch when something downstream (CamillaDSP) expects the real rate.

Add halving stages (63 tap Blackman windowed sinc low pass, cut-off at a
quarter of the input rate, unity gain at DC) after dsd2pcm and pick the
smallest number of halvings that gives a rate in the device's supported
list: 176.4 kHz for DSD64 and DSD128 on a 192 kHz DAC. A device that
accepts DSD rate / 8 keeps it unchanged (no extra stage).

Also scale the 32 bit conversion by -3 dB: DSD masters may peak above
digital full scale ("over") and the plain conversion clipped them.

Source: ralph-irving/squeezelite 72e1fd8abfa9b2f8e9636f033247526920878718
Upstream: Not submitted (local change)

